            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n> \r\n> You can supply file path directly to `MiniFiles.open()` in which case [it will try to focus on that file](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/760c1f3619418f769526884a3de47f0b76245887/doc/mini-files.txt#L628-L629). You can use path of current buffer [like this](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/760c1f3619418f769526884a3de47f0b76245887/doc/mini-files.txt#L616) or [like this](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/760c1f3619418f769526884a3de47f0b76245887/doc/mini-files.txt#L619).\r\n> \r\n> Expanding until current working directory was requested more than once, so I'll think about it. But there isn't high hope for it to be implemented, as it both is somewhat visible in window title full path and can be reached manually by going out. Meanwhile, there is [this custom solution](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/discussions/395#discussioncomment-6305006).\r\n\r\n@echasnovski \r\n\r\nThanks for the custom solution!  But there is a problem with it.  After a while, it starts throwing error:\r\n\r\n```\r\nError executing vim.schedule lua callback: (mini.files) `line` should be a valid line number in buffer 19.\r\nstack traceback:\r\n\t[C]: in function 'error'\r\n\t...vio/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini.files/lua/mini/files.lua:2428: in function 'error'\r\n\t...vio/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini.files/lua/mini/files.lua:2422: in function 'validate_line'\r\n\t...vio/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini.files/lua/mini/files.lua:878: in function 'get_fs_entry'\r\n\t/Users/otavio/.config/nvim/lua/core/mappings.lua:92: in function </Users/otavio/.config/nvim/lua/core/mappings.lua:64>\r\n```\r\n",
